// Generado por tools/build-help.mjs desde docs/ (en). No editar a mano: npm run help.
export default {
 "intro": "<p>A-CAD is a <b>parametric</b> 2D drawing editor for iOS, Android and the browser. You draw points, lines, circles, arcs, ellipses and splines, relate them with <b>constraints</b> (horizontal, parallel, tangent...) and <b>dimensions</b>, and a geometric solver keeps everything consistent: if you move a point or change a measurement, the rest of the drawing rearranges itself. The texts in the drawing <b>calculate</b> (variables, formulas, vectors and matrices) and can drive the dimensions, and mechanisms move by hand or with <b>graphical simulations</b> over time, with actuators and graphs. It is useful for sketches, descriptive geometry, graphic statics, mechanisms and laser-cut parts.</p>",
 "chapters": {
  "conceptos": "<details data-ch=\"conceptos\"><summary>Basic concepts</summary>\n<p>The ideas behind A-CAD. If you have never used a parametric CAD, start here: with these ideas clear, the rest of the manual explains itself.</p>\n<p>Index · <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"empezar\">1. Getting started →</a></p>\n<details><summary>Parametric drawing</summary>\n<p>In an ordinary drawing program, a line is a stroke that stays where you left it. In a <b>parametric</b> drawing, each element also keeps its <b>relationships</b> with the others: this line is horizontal, that circle is tangent to it, this point lies on that arc, this distance measures 40. When you move something or change a measurement, the program repositions everything else so those relationships still hold.</p>\n<p>That is why you work in two steps in A-CAD:</p>\n<ol><li><b>You draw the approximate shape</b>, without worrying about exact measurements.</li><li><b>You add constraints and dimensions</b> until the shape is the way you want it. The exact measurements come from the dimensions, not from your hand.</li></ol>\n<p>The advantage shows up later: to change the design, you just change one dimension, and everything that depends on it adjusts.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Constraints</summary>\n<p>A <b>constraint</b> is a geometric relationship that is always maintained: horizontal, vertical, parallel, perpendicular, coincident, midpoint, concentric, equal, tangent or fixed. You add them by selecting the elements and tapping the constraint's button (see <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"restricciones\">Constraints</a>).</p>\n<p>Many are created automatically while you draw: if a line ends up horizontal or tangent because you accepted the soft snap, the constraint is added (see <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"dibujar\">Drawing</a>). Each constraint carries a number per type (H1, ∥2, T1...), labeled next to its elements and shown with that number in the element tree; only the points that end up on an element while drawing (●) are not labeled.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Degrees of freedom</summary>\n<p>Each element has <b>degrees of freedom</b>: the independent ways it can move. A free point has two (it can move in x and in y). A line has those of its two endpoints. Each constraint and each dimension removes degrees of freedom: a horizontal line can no longer tilt; if you also fix its length with a dimension, it can only translate.</p>\n<p>A drawing in which nothing can move without breaking a relationship is <b>fully defined</b>. You do not need to get there: in a mechanism, precisely, you leave free the movements you care about. To see what is still free, <b>drag</b>: whatever moves still has freedom.</p>\n<p>There are two typical errors:</p>\n<ul><li><b>Conflicting constraints</b>: two relationships that cannot both be satisfied, such as a line that is horizontal and vertical at the same time. The app warns you and undoes the action.</li><li><b>Redundant constraints</b>: a relationship that others already imply. It breaks nothing, but it makes every calculation slower. The \"Redundant\" button finds them (see <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"restricciones\">Constraints</a>).</li></ul>\n</details>\n<details><summary>The solver</summary>\n<p>The <b>solver</b> is the engine that, after each change, looks for positions for all the points that satisfy all the constraints and dimensions at once. A-CAD uses <b>PlaneGCS</b>, the solver of FreeCAD's Sketcher (see <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"acerca\">About</a>).</p>\n<p>The solver starts from where things are and looks for the closest solution. That is why <b>the approximate shape matters</b>: if a tangency can occur on the inside or the outside, the solver picks the one closest to what you drew. If a constraint does not converge, move the elements a little closer to the position you want and try again.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Driving and reference dimensions</summary>\n<p>A <b>dimension</b> is a measurement in the drawing: distance, angle, radius or diameter (see <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"cotas\">Dimensions</a>). It can be of two types:</p>\n<ul><li><b>Driving</b>: it rules over the geometry. If you type 40, the drawing adjusts so that distance measures 40. It removes degrees of freedom, like a constraint.</li><li><b>Reference</b> (read-only, in italics): it only measures. It shows the current value without forcing anything, so it never conflicts. It is useful for reading results, for example how far a point of a mechanism travels.</li></ul>\n<p>Dimensions have names (D1 for distances, A1 for angles, R1 for radii, O1 for diameters) and a dimension can depend on another through a formula: <code>D2 = D1/2</code>. A reference dimension can also feed a formula, so one measurement in the drawing can govern another.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Calculating in the drawing</summary>\n<p>Texts in the drawing can calculate (see <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"calc\">Text and calculation</a>). <code>k:25</code> defines a variable, <code>2*k=</code> shows a result, and dimension formulas can use those variables. There are also vectors, matrices and built-in functions.</p>\n<p>The page reads like a mathematical spreadsheet: <b>from top to bottom and, within the same row, from left to right</b>. A variable has the value of its last definition before the point where it is used, and it can be redefined further down. If you use it before defining it, the app warns you that it is defined further down.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Graphical simulations</summary>\n<p>A mechanism is a drawing with degrees of freedom on purpose: when you drag a link, the solver repositions the others. A <b>simulation</b> does the same but over time (see <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"simulaciones\">Graphical simulations</a>):</p>\n<ol><li>An <b>actuator</b> says how a part moves: a rotation (rotational) or a displacement (linear), with an expression of time <code>t</code> such as <code>90*t</code>, or with a table.</li><li>At each instant, the solver solves the drawing with the actuator in its position.</li><li><b>Graphs</b> record any measurement (a dimension, a point's coordinate) against time or against another measurement, and <b>paths</b> leave the trajectory of a point.</li></ol>\n<p>Gears, rack and pinion, and relationships that hold only while two elements touch (stops, cams) extend what can be simulated.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Glossary</summary>\n<table class=\"fref\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Actuator</td><td>Element that moves a part of the drawing over time: rotational (MR) or linear (ML).</td></tr><tr><td>Layer</td><td>Group of elements that are shown or hidden together.</td></tr><tr><td>Construction</td><td>Helper line, circle or arc: drawn in gray and not included in laser cutting.</td></tr><tr><td>Outline</td><td>Chain of lines, arcs and splines joined at their endpoints; closed if it returns to its start.</td></tr><tr><td>Driving dimension</td><td>Measurement that forces the geometry to have that value.</td></tr><tr><td>Reference dimension</td><td>Measurement that only reads the current value (in italics).</td></tr><tr><td>Degree of freedom</td><td>Independent way in which an element can move.</td></tr><tr><td>Array</td><td>Repeated copies of a selection, in a line or around a center.</td></tr><tr><td>Constraint</td><td>Geometric relationship that the solver always maintains.</td></tr><tr><td>Solver</td><td>Engine that repositions the geometry to satisfy all constraints and dimensions.</td></tr><tr><td>Spline</td><td>Smooth curve that passes through points of the drawing.</td></tr><tr><td>Linked</td><td>Copy that follows the original when it changes.</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>Index · <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"empezar\">1. Getting started →</a></p>\n</details>\n</details>",
  "empezar": "<details data-ch=\"empezar\"><summary>Getting started</summary>\n<p>How to use the app: gestures with the pencil, finger and mouse, the parts of the screen, and how to select.</p>\n<details><summary>Apple Pencil or mouse</summary>\n<p>They draw and select.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Finger</summary>\n<p>Pans the canvas and, with two fingers, zooms (pinch). With <i>Finger draws</i> (\"View\" panel) the finger draws too.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Mouse</summary>\n<p>Wheel = zoom; middle button, right button or Space + drag = pan.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Menu (☰)</summary>\n<p>Shows or hides the tools panel on the right. The first button on the bar opens the <b>element tree</b>.</p>\n<ul><li>The bottom bar explains the active tool and shows the data of the selected element.</li><li>Undo, redo, delete selection and fit view are on the right of the bar.</li><li>Selecting: tap an element (tap several to add). The panel buttons are only enabled if the selection suits them.</li></ul>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Finger magnifier</summary>\n<p>Touch and <b>hold</b> (a quarter of a second, without moving) and a circle appears with a <b>2x zoom</b> of what is under your finger, offset upward so your finger does not cover it; the crosshair in the center marks the exact point. Without lifting, slide your finger to fine-tune. It works with \"Finger draws\" on (any tool) and, with \"Finger draws\" off, in <b>Select</b>: on release, whatever is under the crosshair gets selected. If you move your finger before it appears, it is a normal pan. With \"Finger draws\" and Select, holding means aiming: on release, whatever is under the crosshair gets selected, without moving anything or making a box (the box is made by dragging right away from an empty spot).</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Cursor ring</summary>\n<p>The small circle that accompanies the pencil (over the screen) or the mouse also appears in the Select tool, at the cursor's real position.</p>\n</details>\n</details>",
  "dibujar": "<details data-ch=\"dibujar\"><summary>Drawing</summary>\n<p>The tools that create geometry (points, lines, circles, arcs, ellipses, splines, rectangles and polygons) and the drawing aids: the soft lock and the intersection magnet.</p>\n<p>Buttons with a marked corner are <b>dropdowns</b>: they show the last tool used from the group. Tap the corner, tap the button again when it is already active, or press and hold it to see the others.</p>\n<details><summary>Point (P)</summary>\n<p>On a line or circle, it is constrained to it.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Line (L)</summary>\n<p>Tap the start and the end (it chains) or drag a segment. Esc or Select ends it.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Vector (W)</summary>\n<p><b>Vector (W)</b> (in the Line dropdown): drawn like a line, but it has an <b>arrowhead at the end point</b> (the tail is the start) and its <b>magnitude</b> written as text at the midpoint (the insertion point is the middle of the top side of the box, so it stays centered, and by default it is <b>aligned with the line</b> of the vector: it turns with it and reads from left to right). The magnitude is a named dimension (D1, D2...), read-only at first, so you can use it in formulas and texts. <b>To change it, double-tap the vector</b>: its properties include <b>Magnitude</b> (a value or a formula with other dimensions, such as <code>2*D1</code>; typing it fixes the length), <b>Fixed magnitude</b> (clear it to go back to measuring only) and its <b>Name</b>. The vector's properties also have <b>Unit</b> and <b>Scale</b>: with Magnitude 30, Unit kN and Scale <code>1'mm/kN</code> (or a text variable, <code>eF</code>) the vector measures 30 mm and its text says \"30 kN\", and in formulas and texts the magnitude is worth 30 kN. If the magnitude is not fixed, the unit and the scale say how much the vector's measured length is worth: the resultant of a force polygon is read from the drawing in kN (see <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"cotas\" data-topic=\"dimension-with-unit\">Dimension with unit</a>). <b>Convert</b>: with a line selected, and one of its ends (it will be the tail; if you choose none, the tail is the start), choose Vector in the dropdown and the line becomes a vector; with a vector selected, choose Line and it stops being one (the arrow, the text and the dimension are removed). Deleting the vector also deletes its text; copies and arrays copy it with its own text and its own magnitude. The arrow appears in the SVG and the DXF (not in the laser-cut SVG, which treats it as a line). The magnitude text is a normal text: it can be moved, resized or given another font, or you can release the alignment (Text → Release).</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Midpoint line (M)</summary>\n<p><b>Midpoint line (M)</b> (Line dropdown): tap the start point, which will be the <i>midpoint</i>, and then an end (or drag); the line grows equally to the other side. It gets a midpoint (M) constraint and the soft lock works as it does for lines.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Construction line (K)</summary>\n<p><b>Construction line (K)</b> (Line dropdown): gray (#C3C3C3), \"thin\" weight, with a continuous stroke in the middle and a point at each end, separated by a gap; the points look like those of the \"dash-dot\" type. It is drawn like a normal line, with soft lock. It does not appear in the laser-cut SVG unless you change its class to Cut or Mark.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Circle (C)</summary>\n<p>Center and a point on the edge.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Circle through 3 points</summary>\n<p><b>Circle through 3 points</b> (Circle dropdown): tap 3 non-collinear points; the points end up on the circumference.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Ellipse (O)</summary>\n<p>Tap the center; then tap (or drag to) the end of the first axis and, finally, a point of the second axis (the ellipse passes through the projection of that point onto the perpendicular to the first axis). You can also drag from the center to the end of the first axis. It is an element of its own: it can be selected, copied and exported, it appears in the tree, and its three points (center, axis 1 and axis 2) can be dimensioned and constrained. It can be trimmed, you can extend up to it, and it supports coincident and tangent; it does not support intersections.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Partial ellipse</summary>\n<p><b>Partial ellipse</b> (Ellipse dropdown): creates an elliptical arc. The first three steps are those of the ellipse (center, end of the first axis and point of the second axis); a dotted reference ellipse remains. Then <i>drag over it</i> from where the section starts to where it ends: the section follows the direction of your movement (↻ or ↺). You can also tap the start and then the end (with two taps the short section is taken). The ends are points that slide over the ellipse.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Arc by center and ends (R)</summary>\n<p>Center, start and end. The direction comes from <i>where you move</i>: after tapping the start, move the cursor (or drag with your finger or the pencil) to the side you want and release at the end; the preview shows ↻ (clockwise) or ↺ (counterclockwise). With a direct tap at the end, the short arc is created.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Construction circle and arcs</summary>\n<p><b>Construction circle and arcs</b> (in the Circle and Arc dropdowns): they use the color and weight of the construction line. The circle looks \"dash-dot\"; the arcs have a point at each end. You can also <i>convert</i> any chosen element to the \"Construction\" type in the Style panel (it takes its color and weight).</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Arc by 3 points (A)</summary>\n<p><b>Arc by 3 points (A)</b> (Arc dropdown): start, intermediate point and end.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Spline (S)</summary>\n<p>Tap the points the curve passes through; tapping the last one again (or Esc) ends it, tapping the first one closes it.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Rectangle (B)</summary>\n<p>Tap (or drag between) two opposite corners. It gets horizontal and vertical constraints, so it stays a rectangle when you move a corner.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Center rectangle</summary>\n<p><b>Center rectangle</b> (Rectangle dropdown): tap the center and a corner (or drag from the center to the corner). The center is the midpoint of the diagonal.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Polygon (G)</summary>\n<p>Tap the center and the radius; then choose the number of sides (3 to 64) and whether it is <i>inscribed</i> (the radius reaches a vertex) or <i>circumscribed</i> (the radius reaches the middle of a side). The angle of the radius with the horizontal gives the orientation, with the same soft locks as lines. It stays regular when you move a vertex (vertices equidistant from the center and equal sides, hidden constraints).</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Intersection</summary>\n<p>It no longer has a button in the toolbar; it is still available in the panel (select 2 elements and press Intersection) and with the I key.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Soft lock and intersection magnet when drawing lines</summary>\n<p>When drawing a line, if the cursor is near a notable direction, the line snaps to it and a dotted guide appears with a label:</p>\n<h4>Horizontal, vertical and 45°</h4>\n<p><b>Horizontal, vertical and 45°</b> (every 45°; the step can be changed in the View panel, for example to 15°): the label shows H, V or the angle. H and V add their constraint; the other angles add none.</p>\n<h4>Near an existing line</h4>\n<p><b>Near an existing line</b> (within about 36 px of the cursor): <b>parallel (∥)</b> or <b>perpendicular (⟂)</b> to it, with the corresponding constraint. If the locked direction crosses a line, an arc or a circle near the cursor, the new line <b>ends at that crossing</b>, on that element (the perpendicular ends at the foot of the perpendicular).</p>\n<h4>Near an arc or circle</h4>\n<p><b>tangent (T)</b>, with the end on the curve. Also if you start on a curve.</p>\n<ul><li>If you start on a line or at its end, it also gives parallel and perpendicular to it.</li></ul>\n<h4>Automatic constraints</h4>\n<p>If the line ends up horizontal, vertical, perpendicular, parallel or tangent because you accepted the lock, the corresponding constraint is created with the elements involved (the end that falls on a line or curve stays on it). 45° and the other intermediate angles add no constraint.</p>\n<ul><li>An <b>existing point</b>, an <b>intersection</b> or the <b>midpoint of a line</b> (marked with an M) near the cursor take priority over the lock. The midpoint creates the point with a hidden midpoint constraint, so it stays in the middle if the line changes. It is not offered on lines that are very short on screen.</li></ul>\n<h4>Intersection magnet</h4>\n<p>If the start or the end of the line is near the intersection of two elements (lines, arcs, circles or splines), the line starts or ends there (∩) and the point stays on both. With the cursor, you have to stay close for a few milliseconds (80 by default, configurable in View; 0 = instantly); a tap or the start of a drag takes it instantly.</p>\n<p>If the constraint conflicts with the existing ones, it is removed and the line is left as is. The lock is turned off with <i>Soft lock</i> in the View panel.</p>\n</details>\n</details>",
  "seleccionar": "<details data-ch=\"seleccionar\"><summary>Selecting and editing</summary>\n<p>Selecting (by tap, box or filter), viewing and changing an element's properties, and the tools that modify what you drew: copy, trim, extend, fillet and chamfer.</p>\n<details><summary>Select (V)</summary>\n<p>Selects, drags points, lines or the edge of a circle (the mechanism moves according to the constraints) and, when you drag from an empty spot, selects with a box. A box drawn from left to right takes what ends up entirely inside; from right to left, it also takes what it touches. <b>Graphs</b> are part of the selection (a single one behaves as if you tapped it; several, or together with other elements, are highlighted, deleted and moved along with them).</p>\n<p>With <b>several elements selected</b>, dragging any one of them moves all of them together: their free points (endpoints, centers, control points and the insertion point of texts). Fixed points and reference marks stay where they are, and the constraints still hold, so whatever is tied to something fixed adjusts instead of moving as a whole. With a single element selected, dragging works as usual (the edge of a circle changes its radius).</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Filter</summary>\n<p><b>Filter</b> (in the Select dropdown): chooses what can be selected, by entity (points, lines, circles, arcs, ellipses, splines, texts, fills, dimensions) and by line type. It applies to tapping and to the selection box. A red dot on the button shows that a filter is active.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Box selection</summary>\n<p>With the Select tool, drag from an empty spot. From <b>left to right</b> (blue box, solid line) it selects the elements that end up <b>completely inside</b>; from <b>right to left</b> (orange box, dashed line) it also selects those the box <b>touches</b>. With Shift it adds to the selection. It reaches points, lines, circles, arcs, splines and texts (a text by its insertion point). With a finger, turn on <i>Finger draws</i>, because otherwise the finger pans the canvas.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Overlapping elements</summary>\n<p><b>Overlapping elements</b> (Select tool): when several elements share a spot or sit almost on top of each other (coincident points, centers of concentric circles or arcs, collinear lines), <b>tap and hold</b> on them for about 0.7 s: a floating window opens with a list of all the elements near the tap, from the nearest to the farthest. Check the ones you want: they are highlighted in orange in the drawing so you can identify them. <b>Confirm</b> leaves them selected (anything you already had selected outside that list is kept) and <b>Cancel</b> (or Esc) closes without changing anything. \"All\" / \"None\" checks the whole list. <b>Properties</b> (active when only one element is checked) leaves it selected and opens its properties window without closing the list; it is the way to edit it without an extra tap undoing the selection. You can move the window by dragging its title bar, to clear the work area (it stays where you leave it). With a single element under your finger it does not appear: the usual magnifier follows. It respects the selection filter.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Properties</summary>\n<p><b>Double-tap</b> a point, line, circle, arc, ellipse, spline, text or fill and its properties window opens (on a dimension, the double tap still edits its value). Depending on the element: <b>layer</b>, <b>color</b>, <b>line type</b>, <b>thickness</b> and <b>pattern scale</b>; for a text, all its options: the content, the <b>font color</b> or <b>background color</b> (the same swatches; two radio buttons choose which one), the <b>font</b>, the <b>height</b>, the angle, <b>align with</b> a line, <b>readable</b> and the <b>insertion point</b> (if more texts are selected, the change applies to all of them); for a point, its <b>color</b>, its X and Y coordinates, whether it is fixed and its <b>reference mark</b>; for a <b>vector</b>, its <b>magnitude</b> (a value or a formula; typing it fixes it), whether it is fixed or read-only and its name. It also lists the element's <b>constraints and dimensions</b>, each with \"Remove\", and offers the ones that can be added to that element alone (line: horizontal, vertical, length and fix; circle: diameter and fix; arc: radius). Changes apply immediately and can be undone.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Copy / Paste</summary>\n<p><b>Copy / Paste</b> (dropdown; Ctrl+C and Ctrl+V): the button shows Copy, and after copying it switches to showing Paste (to copy again, use Ctrl+C or the dropdown). Copy stores the selection with its internal constraints; Paste asks for a point and places the center of the box that encloses what you copied at that point (you see the box as you move). You can paste several times; Esc or Select ends it. What you copied is kept even if you delete the original, but it is lost when you close the app.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Trim (X)</summary>\n<p>Press and hold, then drag over the sections you want to remove (between crossings with other elements). A tap trims one section. Only that section is removed: the rest of the element is not split at the other crossings. With no crossings, it deletes the whole element. It also trims <b>ellipses</b> (a full ellipse needs 2 crossings and becomes an elliptical arc; an elliptical arc is shortened or split into two that share center and axes) and <b>splines</b> (each piece is a new spline that passes through the cut, through the original points and through extra points taken from the curve to keep the shape; it is a very close approximation, not an exact one).</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Extend (E)</summary>\n<p>Tap a segment or arc near the end you want to lengthen; it reaches the first element it finds, including ellipses, elliptical arcs and splines. An open spline can also be extended: it is lengthened in a straight line, in the direction of its tangent at that end, and the new end point lands on the element it finds (a spline with relations is not extended). When you extend again, the same end moves on to the next crossing (it leaves no points at the previous ones).</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Fillet (F) and Chamfer</summary>\n<p><b>Fillet (F) and Chamfer</b> (button after Polygon; tap again or press and hold to choose): joins <b>two stroke elements</b> (segments, arcs and elliptical arcs, in any combination; construction lines do not count) with a tangent arc (fillet) or with a line (chamfer). There are three ways to choose them: <b>(1)</b> tap the point of a <b>corner</b> (where two elements end, or two points joined by a coincidence constraint) and type the <b>radius</b> or the chamfer <b>distance</b>; <b>(2)</b> tap one element and then the other, <b>on the side you want to keep</b>, even if they do not meet at a corner (they cross or do not even touch: the fillet lengthens or trims each one up to its tangent point; the chamfer needs them to intersect, either themselves or their extensions); <b>(3)</b> <b>select before choosing the tool</b> one or several corner points (the same value for all of them, for example the four of a rectangle) or exactly two elements (the part toward the end farthest from the vertex is kept). The value can be a formula (<code>R1/2</code>, <code>m*0.3</code>). Of the possible solutions, the arc smaller than a half turn and closest to the vertex wins, regardless of the order in which you choose the elements. The fillet is an arc with its <b>radius dimension</b> (editable: changing it moves the arc; in the arc's properties, the \"Radius\" button edits that same dimension); the chamfer carries one <b>dimension per leg</b> (D1, D2...; they can be different), measured in a straight line from the vertex. At a corner of two lines that share the point, <b>the point is not deleted</b>: it stays as a virtual vertex on the two straight lines, and the dimensions and constraints that used it still hold; between other elements, the vertex (if they intersect) stays as a hidden virtual point. When a segment is trimmed this way, its length dimensions and its length equality are removed (they no longer hold) and you are notified. With loose elements, changing the radius may let the solver deform them: it is best to fix or dimension whatever must not move. If the value does not fit, you are warned. A fill that used the two elements includes the arc or the chamfer. Circles, full ellipses (trim the ellipse first) and vectors cannot be filleted.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Locked texts and images</summary>\n<p><b>Locked texts and images</b>: a tap <b>does not select a text</b> (in documents with many texts they were grabbed and moved by accident): a tap <b>edits it in place</b>, with the same input as the Text tool placed over it and the cursor where you tapped (an equation without its <code>$$</code>, a normal text as it is, a script with its code). Enter saves (in a script, Shift+Enter), Shift+Enter makes a new line, tapping outside also saves and Esc leaves it as it was; if what you typed gives an error, you are warned and the input stays open so you can fix it. A collapsed code object (an inserted library, or a script without \"Show the code in the drawing\") is not edited this way: the tap passes through to whatever is underneath (a line, a point); it is opened with a double tap, in its properties. To select a text, <b>enclose it with a box</b>, dragging from it or from an empty spot (from left to right it must end up entirely inside; from right to left, touching it is enough); once selected, you drag it to move it. The <b>double tap</b> still opens its properties, and a tap on a <b>link</b> selects the text and shows the pill with its destination (without opening it). An image has an <b>insertion point</b>, like a text: a real point that shows when you select it, accepts constraints and can be read in formulas (<code>p12.x</code>); in its properties you choose its anchor (9 positions), and when you enlarge or shrink it that point stays fixed. A <b>locked image</b> (\"Locked\" checkbox in its properties) follows the same rule: tapping and dragging ignore it, and the double tap opens its properties so you can unlock it.</p>\n</details>\n</details>",
  "dividir": "<details data-ch=\"dividir\"><summary>Splitting, copying and repeating</summary>\n<p>Splitting an element into sections and repeating geometry with linear arrays, circular arrays and mirror copies, linked to the original or not.</p>\n<details><summary>Split</summary>\n<p><b>Split</b> (panel, Dimensions section...): with 1 segment, arc or circle plus the points that are on it, it cuts it at those points. With no points, it divides it into <b>n equal parts</b> (on lines, the parts get an equal-length constraint; on arcs and circles there is no equal-angle constraint).</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Linear array</summary>\n<p>Select what you want to copy. Define direction 1 (with a line: from its start to its end; with no line, horizontal), the spacing and the <i>total number</i> (it includes the original; if you only give the spacing, the total is 2). Direction 2 is optional and fills a grid; with no line it is perpendicular to 1. The chosen directions are highlighted with an arrow on the canvas.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Circular array</summary>\n<p>Center (origin or a point you tap), total angle (360° by default), total number, equal spacing or angle between instances, reversed direction and <i>skip instances</i> (for example 3, 5).</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Variables in Split and in the arrays</summary>\n<p>The fields of these tools (number of parts, spacing, total number, angles and skipped instances) accept dimensions and the <code>x:value</code> variables defined in the texts, as well as numbers and formulas. In the fields that ask for an <b>integer</b> (number of parts, total number, skipped instances), a variable or formula is rounded to the nearest integer, like <code>round(variable;0)</code>; a plain number must be an integer. The dialogs have a <b>variables menu</b> with a filter (all, dimensions, angles, text variables): choosing one inserts its name into the field where you were typing, at the cursor position.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Mirror copy</summary>\n<p>Select what you want to reflect, press the button and tap the line that acts as the axis. Points on the axis are shared.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Linked copies</summary>\n<p><b>Linked copies</b> (option \"Link the copies to the original\", on by default): each point of the copy is tied to its original with automatic constraints, so the copy is a rigid replica that <b>follows the original</b> if you move it, rotate it or change its size. In the linear array, the distance and the direction (the angle relative to the chosen line, or absolute if there is no line); in the circular array, equal distance to the center and the angle at the center; in the mirror copy, symmetry about the axis and, for circles, equal radius. <b>The array dimensions are optional</b> (\"Show the array dimensions\"): the distance between the original and the first copy in the linear array, and the angular dimension in the circular one. With the option off, the copies are independent and carry only their internal constraints.</p>\n</details>\n</details>",
  "restricciones": "<details data-ch=\"restricciones\"><summary>Constraints</summary>\n<p>The geometric relations the solver always maintains: horizontal, parallel, tangent, coincident and the rest, with their numbering in the drawing.</p>\n<p>Select the elements and press the button. Each button states what selection it needs.</p>\n<details><summary>Horizontal / Vertical</summary>\n<p>Lines, or 2 points.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Distribute ↔ / Distribute ↕</summary>\n<p>3 or more points or texts (a text, by its insertion point). They end up <b>evenly spaced</b> horizontally (x) or vertically (y), between the two at the ends (the leftmost and the rightmost, or the lowest and the highest), which do not move when you apply it. The other coordinate stays free: to align them as well, use Horizontal or Vertical. It is a constraint like the others: the spacing is an unknown, so if you drag an end point the ones in between are spread out again, and if you drag one in between they all move. It is labeled ↔1, ↕1 next to each point.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Parallel / Perpendicular</summary>\n<p>2 or more lines.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Coincident</summary>\n<p>2 or more points (they end up in the same place); point(s) and 1 line, arc, circle, ellipse or spline (the points end up on the element); or lines/arcs and 1 ellipse or spline (the nearest end of each line or arc ends up on the curve). <b>Collinear</b>: 2 or more lines.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Midpoint</summary>\n<p>1 point + 1 line.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Concentric (◎)</summary>\n<p>2 or more circles or arcs; the center of the first one rules and the other centers coincide with it. If they already share the center, you are warned and nothing is added.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Equal</summary>\n<p>2 lines (equal length) or 2 circles/arcs (equal radius). When you fillet or chamfer a corner, the length equality of the lines that get shortened becomes an equality of distances between their other end and the virtual vertex of the corner (it shows as \"=\" next to those points).</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Tangent</summary>\n<p>Line + circle/arc, 2 circles/arcs, a spline with a line, circle or arc, an <b>ellipse</b> (or elliptical arc) with a line, circle or arc, or 2 ellipses. With ellipses and splines the contact point appears.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Fix / release</summary>\n<p>Points, lines or circles. A fixed point does not move when you drag.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Reference mark</summary>\n<p><b>Reference mark</b> (properties of a point, \"Reference mark at\" and the line): a mark <b>is not a fixed point</b>. The solver does not move it while solving, but after each change it is <b>projected onto its line</b>, which is already in its new position and orientation. This way it slides along the line by itself, carried by it, and <b>remembers the distance traveled</b>: the distance between the mark and another point on the line accumulates the path. A position dimension cannot do this, because it returns to the same value when a trip is closed; the mark can. It is drawn as an <b>x</b>. <b>Reset marks</b> (button in the Spline group, or \"Reset\" in the point's properties) returns each mark to its <i>reference</i>: the distance along the line, from its first end, that it had when it was assigned. It is useful for placing the mechanism somewhere else and starting the reading again from zero. \"Take the current one\" changes the reference. It is useful for rails, wheels that roll on a rotating line and planimeters. Example, the polar planimeter: the pole and the arm with the tracer P follow the outline of a figure; the rail (perpendicular to the arm) passes through P and carries a mark; D = distance from the mark to P (read-only), the wheel's rotation is <code>(D-D0)/(O1/2)</code> and the area is <code>arm length × radius × rotation</code>. A mark belongs to a single line and cannot be an end of it; if the line is deleted, it stops being a mark; copies and arrays reassign it to the copy of its line. <b>Limit</b>: the mark advances in steps (each solve), so the accumulated travel has an error that decreases with finer drags (in the planimeter, 2 to 4 % with a normal drag).</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Indicators</summary>\n<p>Each relation is labeled with its letter and number next to the elements it involves (the same number and letter on all of them): one between a point and a line, circle or arc, next to the point and the element; a horizontal or vertical between 2 points, next to both. Gear relations are marked with <b>G</b> and wheel-and-rack ones with <b>Cr</b>.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Redundant constraints</summary>\n<p>A redundant constraint adds no information (others already imply the same thing) and slows down every calculation, because the solver solves twice and diagnoses them. The \"Redundant\" button (Constraints group) lists them and offers to remove them; when you open a file that has them, a notice appears. Named dimensions are not removed automatically (they may feed formulas).</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Numbering</summary>\n<p>Each constraint carries a number per type (H1, H2, ⟂1, ∥1, =1, ●1, T1...). Those that involve several elements share a single number and are labeled next to each element (for example, two perpendicular lines each show ⟂1). The number is not reused if you delete the constraint. In the element tree each constraint appears with its number: tap it to select it together with its elements and bring the canvas to it. The automatic constraints that place a point on an element while drawing (●) are not labeled.</p>\n<p>If a constraint contradicts the others, the app warns you and undoes the action.</p>\n</details>\n</details>",
  "cotas": "<details data-ch=\"cotas\"><summary>Dimensions</summary>\n<p>Dimensions: measurements that drive the geometry or only read it, with formulas, names, scale and units.</p>\n<p>The full list of functions and operators, with examples, is in <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"funcs\">Functions and operators</a>.</p>\n<details><summary>Dimension (D)</summary>\n<p><b>Dimension (D)</b> (after Text): a single tool for all dimensions. Tap the elements and the dimension is created automatically once the selection is complete: <b>2 points</b> or <b>a point and a line</b> give the distance; <b>2 parallel lines</b> (less than 1° apart), the distance between them; <b>2 non-parallel lines</b>, the angle; <b>an arc</b>, the radius; <b>a circle</b>, the diameter; and <b>an arc or circle with another element</b> (a point, a line, another arc or circle, or an ellipse), the distance from that element to the center. To dimension an element against an arc or circle, tap the other element first and then the arc or circle (an arc alone, tapped first, gives its radius), or select them before choosing the tool. If something was already selected when you chose Dimension, it dimensions that selection (a single line gives its length). Tap an element again to remove it from the selection; Esc clears it.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Distance</summary>\n<p>2 points, 1 line, point + line, <b>arc or circle + another element</b> (distance from that element to the center), or <b>2 parallel lines</b> (perpendicular distance between them; if they did not have the Parallel constraint, it is added, unless the dimension is a reference one; the lines must be parallel within 1°).</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Angle</summary>\n<p>2 lines. The value has no limit (250°, 400° = one full turn and 40°; see <a href=\"#\" data-help=\"cotas\" data-topic=\"angles-without-limit\">Angles without limit</a> below). Where you place it decides which sector is dimensioned (for example 20° or 160°). Drag the dimension to reposition it.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Radius / ⌀</summary>\n<p>Arc (R) or circle (diameter, with the ⌀ symbol). They follow the ANSI standard: with the text inside, the dimension line runs through the interior; with the text outside, a radial leader with the arrow touching from outside.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Scaled dimension</summary>\n<p><b>Scaled dimension</b> (distances, radii and diameters): type <code>value[*k]</code> in the dimension's value, e.g. <code>4[*10]</code>. The <b>drawing measures value × k</b> (here 40 units) and the <b>dimension says the value</b> (\"4\"), <u>underlined</u> as in ISO 129 / UNE 1-039; with <code>4[*0.1]</code> the drawing measures 0.4 and the dimension still says \"4\". The value can be a formula (<code>D1/2[*10]</code>) and <b>so can the scale</b>: a number, a text variable (<code>30[*k]</code> with <code>k:25</code> in a text) or a formula with dimensions (<code>4[*2*D1]</code>); if the variable changes, the drawing is recalculated and the dimension still says 30. <b>Read-only</b> dimensions work too: when you check \"Read-only\" the <b>Scale (×)</b> field appears (empty = no scale) and the dimension says the <b>measurement ÷ scale</b>, underlined and in italics (a measurement of 13.333 with scale 10 says \"1.333\"). The scale is shown when you edit the dimension (<code>value[*k]</code>, or in its field) and disappears when you delete it. It does not apply to angles.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Dimension with unit</summary>\n<p><b>Dimension with unit</b> (distances, radii and diameters): type the value with its unit, <code>126'in</code>. The <b>drawing measures the real thing</b> (a length in mm: <code>126'in</code> measures 3200.4; another SI quantity: <code>5'kN</code> measures 5000, <code>12'V</code> measures 12) and the <b>dimension says it in its unit</b>: \"126 in\". In the dimension's window, <b>Dimension unit</b> shows and changes the unit: with a number it converts it (<code>126'in</code> becomes <code>3200,4'mm</code>); with a formula it only changes the unit at the end (<code>D2*2'ft</code>). A formula with no unit at the end says the unit of a dimension in the formula with the same quantity (<code>D2*2</code> with D2 in in says in) or the SI one. To see it smaller or larger, use the scale: <code>126'in[*0.1]</code> draws 320.04 and the dimension still says \"126 in\", underlined. In a <b>read-only</b> dimension, the unit only changes the label (a measurement of 254 mm, with the unit in, says \"10 in\").</p>\n<p><b>Scale with units</b> (representation scale): for a quantity that is not a length, the scale carries units and the quantity times the scale gives the drawing length: <code>30'kN[*1'mm/kN]</code> measures 30 mm and the dimension says \"30 kN\", <b>not underlined</b> (it is not out of scale, it is at the scale you declared). The scale can be a text variable, <code>eF:1'mm/kN</code>, and each dimension or vector uses it with <code>[*eF]</code>: it reads the eF defined earlier in reading order, so if you redefine it further down (<code>eF:10'mm/kN</code>) the scale of everything below changes. If the quantity times the scale does not give a length (<code>30'kN[*2'mm]</code>) it warns with ⚠. Without a scale, <code>30'kN</code> measures 30000 (in SI) and the app warns you. A <b>read-only</b> dimension with a unit of another quantity (kN) needs a scale with units to read the measurement: it says \"⚠ kN\" until it has one; with it, it says the measurement divided by the scale (45 mm with <code>1'mm/kN</code> says \"45 kN\").</p>\n<p>In formulas and calculation texts a dimension with a unit enters as a <b>quantity</b>: what its label says, with its quantity and without the scale. With D2 = 126 in and D4 = 20 in, <code>A:D2*D4=</code> gives the area (in m²; <code>A='ft^2</code> asks for it in ft²). A dimension without a unit still enters as a number: to add it to one with a unit, give it the unit in the formula, <code>D1'mm+D2</code> (<code>D1+D2</code> gives ⚠).</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Read-only (reference)</summary>\n<p>It measures and shows the value in italics without constraining the geometry.</p>\n<ul><li>Tap a dimension once to <b>select</b> it (for example to delete it), <b>drag</b> it to reposition it, and <b>tap twice</b> to edit its value.</li></ul>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Formulas</summary>\n<p>Any dimension can take an expression: <code>3π/4</code>, <code>2π*5</code>, <code>10*sin(30)</code>, <code>10+1/3</code>, <code>√2</code>, <code>2^3</code>. The same applies inside the <code>expr=</code> outputs and the <code>x:...</code> assignments of the texts. <b>Ranges</b> (vectors of evenly spaced numbers): <code>x:start..end;n</code> defines <code>x</code> with <code>n</code> elements between <code>start</code> and <code>end</code> (both included); <code>x:start..step..end</code> with <code>start</code>, <code>start+step</code>... without going past <code>end</code> (the step can be negative). Each part can be a formula. <code>x=</code> shows the elements; a vector cannot yet be used inside a numeric formula (it warns). <b>Operators</b>: <code>+ - * / ^</code> (also <code>×</code>, <code>÷</code>, <code>**</code>) and implicit multiplication (<code>2π</code>, <code>3(4+1)</code>). <b>Constants</b>: <code>π</code> (or <code>pi</code>) and <code>e</code>. <b>Single-value functions</b>: <code>sin</code> (or <code>sen</code>), <code>cos</code>, <code>tan</code>, <code>sec</code>, <code>csc</code>, <code>cot</code> (in degrees, or in radians if the argument contains π or is an angle), <code>asin</code>, <code>acos</code>, <code>atan</code> (return degrees), <code>sinh</code>, <code>cosh</code>, <code>tanh</code>, <code>asinh</code>, <code>acosh</code>, <code>atanh</code>, <code>sqrt</code> (or <code>raiz</code>, <code>√</code>), <code>abs</code>, <code>exp</code>, <code>ln</code>, <code>log</code> (or <code>log10</code>), <code>deg(x)</code> (radians to degrees), <code>rad(x)</code> (degrees to radians), <code>floor</code> (<code>piso</code>), <code>ceil</code> (<code>techo</code>), <code>trunc</code> and <code>sign</code> (<code>signo</code>). <b>Multi-value functions</b>, separated with <code>;</code> (or with a comma followed by a space or after a variable; <code>2,5</code> is the number 2.5): <code>round(x; n)</code> (<code>redondear</code>; <code>n</code> decimals, 0 if omitted; halves round away from zero), <code>min(a; b; ...)</code>, <code>max(a; b; ...)</code>, <code>clamp(x; mínimo; máximo)</code>, <code>mod(a; b)</code> (the result takes the sign of <code>b</code>), <code>hypot(a; b; ...)</code>, <code>atan2(y; x)</code> (degrees). <b>Comparisons</b>: <code>&lt; &gt; &lt;= &gt;= == !=</code> (also <code>≤ ≥ ≠</code>) give 1 if true and 0 if not (<code>==</code> and <code>!=</code> tolerate rounding), and <code>if(condición; si_verdadero; si_falso)</code> (or <code>si</code>) chooses between two values: it only calculates the chosen branch, so <code>if(D1==0; 0; 10/D1)</code> does not fail. The names of these functions cannot be used as a dimension or variable name, nor can <code>t</code> (time), <code>j</code>, <code>ω</code>, <code>θ</code> or <code>α</code> (only those lowercase letters), which are reserved for future engines, animations and dynamic calculations; for now they have no value.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Dimension names and formulas between dimensions</summary>\n<p>Each dimension has an automatic name by type: <b>D</b> distance, <b>A</b> angle, <b>R</b> arc radius and <b>O</b> circle diameter (D1, D2, A1, R1, O1...; the number is not reused). You see it in the element tree and when you tap the dimension twice, where you can also <b>change it</b> (letters, numbers and _; the new name is updated in the formulas that use it). A dimension's name can be used in another's formula: <code>D1*sin(3π/4)</code>, <code>D1/2+1</code>, <code>2*O1</code>. When you change a dimension's value, the ones that depend on it are recalculated automatically and the geometry is solved again; if the result is impossible (a conflict, or a distance or radius that would end up zero or negative) everything is undone and you are warned. Rules: an <b>angle</b> enters the formula as its measure in radians (like π): <code>sin(A1)</code> works and <code>R1*A1</code> is the arc length; <b>read-only</b> (reference) dimensions can be read, see below; there can be no cycles between driving dimensions (D1 depends on D2 and D2 on D1); a diameter (O) is worth the diameter, not the radius. If a dimension that another depended on is deleted, the latter keeps its current value and the app warns you.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Read-only dimensions as inputs</summary>\n<p>The formula of a driving dimension can read a reference dimension, that is, a measurement of the geometry (the length of a line, an angle, a radius). Since the measurement depends on the geometry and the geometry on the dimension, the app <b>solves and recalculates in rounds</b> until the formula matches the measurement (secant method, and if it does not close, damped repetition). This happens when editing a dimension, when creating one and <b>while you drag</b>, so the output dimension moves with the drawing. So that the output does not push the input, <b>the points measured by the read-only dimensions that the formulas read are kept fixed</b> while solving (in an involute, the chord D1 = R·A1 gets longer without moving the tangent point that A1 measures); if the drawing needs them to move, the app retries without fixing them. When you drag a mechanism with formulas that read measurements, a point on a circle that the mouse cannot reach exactly is pulled in on its own in the first calculation pass. If it does not stabilize (for example, a dimension defined as its own measurement + 1) it is undone and you are warned. Example: the wheel that rolls without slipping. <b>The tangent point T governs</b> (the input): D1 (read-only) measures the distance from the origin to T and A1 (driving, in radians) is worth <code>D1/(O1/2)</code>. When you drag T along the ground, the wheel turns as much as it advances and the marked point P, which stays as the output, traces the cycloid, over several turns. It is best for the input to be the most stable measurement (the position of T) and the output the calculated one; doing it the other way round (dragging P and having the distance read the angle) works, but is ill-conditioned near the ground. <b>Limit</b>: a very fast drag may stop converging at some step (the last good state is kept).</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Angles without limit</summary>\n<p>An angular dimension no longer stays between 0° and 180°. Type 250° and the geometry ends up at 250° (the arc is drawn beyond 180°); 400° is one full turn and 40° (the arc shows the 40°). It is measured in the direction (counterclockwise or clockwise) in which the dimension was created. A <b>read-only</b> angular dimension follows the geometry without jumping: going from 180° to 190°, to 360° and to 400° it keeps growing, and on the way back it goes down again; to do this it takes the equivalent closest to the last value, so it stays correct as long as each change is less than half a turn. Old drawings keep their 0° to 180° value until you edit that dimension (by tapping it twice) or drag it to another sector, and from then on it is continuous. In formulas an angle enters in radians, also beyond 2π.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Degrees or radians</summary>\n<p>Angular dimensions have a unit selector. In degrees, sin/cos/tan use degrees (radians if the argument contains π); in radians, everything is in radians. Dimension formulas always read degrees; the <b>calculation texts</b> and the graphs follow the <b>Settings → Angles in</b> option (radians in new drawings, degrees in earlier ones).</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Point color</summary>\n<p>Tap a point twice and choose its color in the properties window (palette or free color; \"auto\" goes back to the normal color). The color is only visible on screen: points do not appear in exports.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Free label</summary>\n<p><b>Free label</b>: at the end of a dimension's value, <code>[\"texto\"]</code> changes what it says: <code>50[\"D\"]</code> measures 50 and drives the drawing, but reads \"D\". With <code>[$fórmula$]</code> or <code>[$$fórmula$$]</code> the label is drawn with the text engine (subscripts, Greek letters, fractions): <code>50[$D/2$]</code>, <code>8[$r_1$]</code>. It combines with the scale, in that order: <code>30[*1'mm/kN][\"F\"]</code>. When you edit the dimension it is shown in full; to remove it, delete the bracket. In the SVG and the DXF a label with a formula comes out as plain text.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Dimension font size</summary>\n<p><b>Dimension font size</b>: with <b>Settings → Dimensions use the text font size</b> (on in new documents), the dimension font is measured like that of the texts: in <b>points</b> with a page and in drawing units without one, and dimensions grow and shrink with the zoom along with the drawing, also when exporting. When unchecked (earlier drawings), the font is in screen pixels and does not change with the zoom. It is chosen in <b>Style → Dimension text</b> (with no dimensions selected it changes the document's, which all dimensions without their own follow; with dimensions selected, theirs) and in each dimension's window (double tap), under <b>Dimension text</b>. Arrows and spacing grow with the font. Arrowheads follow the ASME Y14.2 standard: their length is the height of the digits and their base is a third of the length (3:1), filled. Leaders use the same arrow as the document's dimensions.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Leaders</summary>\n<p><b>Leaders</b> (annotations): with the dimension tool (or the Distance button), choose <b>a point and the element it belongs to</b>; its window opens. If the point is an <b>endpoint</b> of a line, an arc, an elliptical arc or an open spline, <b>the element itself becomes the leader</b>: the arrow goes at that endpoint, in the direction of the curve; its color, line type and thickness are the element's (change them in the Style panel, with the leader or the element selected), and the element does not appear in the laser-cut SVG. If the point is the center of a circle or arc, or an intermediate point, a straight line of its own is drawn with the arrow at the point and the panel's style. The <b>label is a text of the drawing</b>: its height is in drawing units, like that of the texts, and its arrow is the same as the document's dimensions. In its window (when creating it and with a double tap) you choose the label (plain text, <code>\"texto\"</code>, <code>$fórmula$</code> or <code>$$fórmula$$</code>), <b>where it goes</b> (on a line, an arc or an elliptical arc: an endpoint or the midpoint; on a spline, any of its points), the <b>insertion point</b> of its box (corners, midpoints or center, as in the texts; it is kept a quarter of its height away from the point), the <b>font</b> and the <b>height</b> (with a page, in points, like the texts; it starts with the font and height of new texts). You can also drag the label: it jumps to the nearest allowed place. If the geometry moves, it goes with it. It constrains nothing: the solver does not see it, and it is deleted with its point or its element (deleting it leaves the element). It appears in the SVG and the DXF, with the formula drawn.</p>\n</details>\n</details>",
  "splines": "<details data-ch=\"splines\"><summary>Splines and paths (cams and trajectories)</summary>\n<p>Free curves that pass through points of the drawing, the path of a moving point and offset curves, the basis of cams and trajectories.</p>\n<ul><li>A spline is a <b>cubic B-spline</b> that passes through real points of the drawing. Open: zero curvature at the ends. Closed: periodic, smooth all the way around, with no corner at the joint. Those points behave as free points until you constrain them.</li></ul>\n<details><summary>It is a first-class element</summary>\n<p>It can have points on it, intersections and tangencies with lines, circles and arcs. The solver resolves them exactly, not approximately, together with the rest of the drawing.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Deformation</summary>\n<p>Dragging a point of the spline, or a point attached to it, deforms the curve and the rest of the drawing follows. A follower (a point on the spline and on a straight line, for example) runs along the profile while you move the line.</p>\n<ul><li>A point attached to an <b>open</b> spline cannot go beyond its ends: if you try, the app warns of a conflict and undoes the move.</li><li>When you release a drag, a spline with relations recalculates its internal parameters so that it does not ripple if the points moved a lot; its shape may change slightly.</li></ul>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Spline button</summary>\n<p><b>Spline button</b> (toolbar): tap to draw a spline; the dropdown (tap again or press and hold) contains Trace point, Clear trace, Trace to spline, Close or open and Offset. Trace to spline and Offset ask you for the number of points or the distance when you use them.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Trace point</summary>\n<p>Makes a point draw its path as the mechanism moves. <b>Trace to spline</b> converts the path (the first turn) into an editable spline.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Offset</summary>\n<p>A curve parallel to a spline at a given distance (for example the roller radius of a cam). It warns you if the distance exceeds the radius of curvature.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Close / open</summary>\n<p><b>Close / open</b> the selected spline. In the cut SVG and in the DXF they come out as exact curves.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Fixed shape of a spline</summary>\n<p><b>Fixed shape of a spline</b> (spline properties): ties its control points to each other with hidden distances, so that the curve moves and turns like a rigid body and <b>does not deform</b> when something drags one of its points (for example, the profile of a gear tooth that turns with its wheel). Clear it to edit the curve point by point again. Use it before copying the spline in an array.</p>\n</details>\n</details>",
  "simulaciones": "<details data-ch=\"simulaciones\"><summary>Graphical simulations</summary>\n<p>Moving the drawing over time: rotary and linear actuators, the simulation player, graphs of measurements, gears, rack and pinion, and relations that only hold while the elements touch.</p>\n<details><summary>Actuators</summary>\n<p><b>Actuators</b> (group after Fillet): <b>MR</b> (rotary) is defined by choosing the line and the end of it that will be the center of rotation (tap them in any order, or select them before choosing the tool; it turns counterclockwise by default, or clockwise, and the direction is shown by the circular arrow); <b>ML</b> (linear) with the tail and the head (tap the two points, or select them first: the first one is the tail). You give them a <b>displacement</b> or a <b>speed</b> with respect to time <code>t</code>, through an expression (<code>90*t</code>, <code>30*sin(2π*t)</code>) or a \"time; value\" table. Tap their label, or their name in the element tree (group \"Actuators\"), to edit or delete them; the selection filter can turn off \"Actuators and graphs\". <b>Simulation</b> opens the time bar: it plays and moves the drawing by solving the constraints (the MR line cannot be fixed horizontal or vertical, nor its length changed in the ML); the player has step back/forward, loop repeat (↻), back-and-forth (⇄) and speed from 0.5x to 2.0x; when you stop, the drawing returns to how it was.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Graphs</summary>\n<p><b>Graphs</b> (actuator group): tap where the lower left corner goes and type the relations \"variable1 vs variable2\", one per line (the first goes on the vertical axis): <code>MR1 vs t</code>, <code>MR1.v vs t</code>, <code>D1 vs t</code>, or two vectors of the same size, by name or with an expression (with <code>x:0..1;10</code> and <code>f(x):x.^2</code>: <code>f(x) vs x</code>, <code>x.^2 vs x</code>, <code>sin(x) vs x</code>). Variables: <code>t</code>, the actuators (<code>MR1</code>, <code>ML1</code>; their speed with <code>.v</code>, for example <code>ML1.v</code>), the named dimensions, the variables of the texts and the vectors. Each side accepts operations and functions with those variables: <code>p1.x/p1.y vs t</code>, <code>ML1.d-p1.x vs t</code>. The graph is read at its place on the page, in the <b>reading order</b> (from top to bottom and, within a row, from left to right, by its upper left corner), like a dimension with a formula: it sees the variables and functions defined <b>before</b> it, and what is defined or redefined further down does not affect it. The <b>x</b> belongs to each graph: if the drawing already defines <code>x</code> before the graph (a range or a vector), those values are used one by one; if not, the graph uses a generic vector of <i>Samples</i> values between the limits of its <b>x1</b> axis (from −10 to 10 if you do not set them), so <code>sin(x)/x vs x</code> is drawn without defining anything. What you see is what gets sampled: with <code>x'cm</code> and the axis from 0 to 900 mm, x goes from 0 to 90 cm. With no limits on <b>x1</b> and an x with units, the graph <b>focuses itself</b>: the window goes from 0 to the largest of the measurements with that magnitude that the relation reads, also inside its functions (with a beam of L = 900 mm, from 0 to 900 mm). If there are no such measurements (an x without units, or cuts written as numbers, <code>fsn(x, 0.5, 0)</code>), the window is the stretch where the curve does something (jumps, kinks or curvature): outside it, the curve is constant or straight. A curve that does something over the whole range (<code>sin(x)/x</code>, <code>x^2</code>) stays from −10 to 10. With no limits on y, the y window is that of what is seen in the x window. The time <code>t</code> is defined only by the simulation player (its \"to\"): the graph no longer has an end time. With the graph selected (a tap, or when dragging it), controls appear on its sides; unselected, it looks clean, only the graph: four elastic sliders, at the top (horizontal pan, ⇆), on the right (vertical pan, ⇅), at the bottom (horizontal zoom, ↔) and on the left (vertical zoom, ↕): while you hold them away from the center the window moves (to the right or upward, or the opposite) or zooms in (right or up) or out, faster the further you move them (the pan is slow near the center, for fine adjustment), and when you release them they return to the center; a double tap on a slider returns that axis to the <b>saved view</b>. To save it, leave the graph at the position and zoom you want and tap the round button in the corner, between the zoom sliders (it fills in and saves the limits of x1 and y1); afterwards you can move and zoom freely and the double tap recovers it. A double tap on that button deletes the saved view; without it, the double tap on a slider returns the axis to automatic limits. The <b>⤢ Fit</b> button in the upper right corner puts all the axes in automatic limits with one tap (the saved view does not change). Moving or zooming the window changes the axis limits (also in the graph's properties) and a generic x is recalculated within them. At the end of the line, <code>@y2</code>, <code>@x2</code> or <code>@x2 y2</code> draw it on the secondary axes (x1 bottom, x2 top, y1 left, y2 right). <b>Style of each relation</b>, in quotes at the end: color (<code>r g b c m y k w</code> or <code>#rrggbb</code>; <code>k</code> is the text color), line (<code>-</code> solid, <code>--</code> dashed, <code>:</code> dotted, <code>-.</code> dash-dot) and symbol (<code>o + * . x s d ^ v &gt; &lt; p h</code>: circle, plus, asterisk, point, cross, square, diamond, triangles, star and hexagon). <code>σ vs N \"rx\"</code> is a red cross with no line, <code>y vs x \"k--d\"</code> black dashed with diamonds and <code>Y vs X \"o\"</code> only circles: a <b>scatter plot</b>, to see how data are distributed (for example, from <code>randn</code>). With a symbol and no line, the line is not drawn; without quotes, a solid line in the color that comes next in order. In the graph's properties, below the relations, each one has its lists of color, line and symbol, which write that style. A relation of a single point without a symbol is marked with a circle. Tap a graph to select it (it is highlighted): drag it to move it and drag its edges or corners to resize it. A double tap opens its properties: title, relations, samples, position, size and, for each axis, its free name (for example <code>p12.x [cm]</code>, which serves as documentation because the axes do not show units), linear or logarithmic scale, minimum and maximum, and major and minor divisions. Points with \"Trace point\" draw their trajectory when the simulation runs and keep it when you stop it.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Gear</summary>\n<p><b>Gear</b> (Constraints group): select 2 lines, one rigid with each gear (for example the radius of each one that starts at the fixed center) and type the rotation ratio (negative if they turn in opposite directions; it accepts a formula, e.g. <code>-17/34</code>; with equal modules it is −z1/z2, or the ratio of the pitch or base diameters, not of the outside ones). First position the gears by hand, with the teeth not interfering, and then create the relation: the pose at that moment is the starting point and from then on the rotation of both is governed by the ratio. Drag any point of one of the gears and the other turns with the ratio, for several turns. Conditions: each gear turns around a fixed point (the end of the line that coincides with it) and its points are tied to the center, like those of a circular array. It does not model tooth contact. If you change <code>z</code> or <code>m</code>, position again and create the relation again.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Rack</summary>\n<p><b>Rack</b> (wheel and rack, Constraints group): select, in this order, the radial line of the wheel, the line of the rack (or the ground) and the point that advances, and type the ratio: how far the point advances for each radian the wheel turns (signed; the radius of the wheel. A wheel rolling to the right on the ground turns clockwise, so the ratio is −radius). For a wheel rolling without slipping, the point is its <b>center</b> (not a point on the rim: the app checks this and, if you chose the end of the wheel line at the rim, it uses the other end); for a pinion with its rack, a point of the rack. Drag the wheel or the point and the other moves with the ratio. The pinion must turn around a fixed point (or one tied to a fixed point with \"Coincident\"). The rack line gives the direction of advance. It does not model tooth contact.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Finite relations</summary>\n<p><b>Finite relations</b> (\"Only within limits\", in the properties of an element, in the list of its constraints): by default a tangency, a point on a line or on an arc holds even if the elements are not seen in contact (for the solver, lines and circles are infinite). With the box checked, the relation only holds while the elements <b>touch graphically</b>: their contact point falls within the limits (the segment between its ends, the sweep of the arc) and the separation is almost zero; outside that, the elements are left free. If they cross each other within a step, they are captured. With <b>Stop at edges</b> (it appears when you check \"Only within limits\") the element is not left free when it goes out through an edge: it stops there (the point at the end of the segment or of the arc; in a tangency, the end of the segment or of the arc rests on the other element) and the relation resumes when it goes back inside. It is useful for slots and contacts that begin and end: a Geneva drive is built with a finite \"point on line\" relation between the pin and each slot (the drive advances one slot per turn of the driver). With ideal geometry (a/d = sin(π/n)) the pin enters head-on through the mouth; with any other, it enters from the side and the drive makes a jump when capturing it. Available in: point on line, point on circle or arc, line-circle or arc tangent and tangent between arcs.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Libraries</summary>\n<p><b>Libraries</b>: a document with functions, scripts and tables that you want to use in others. In <b>Settings → Library</b> check \"It is a library\" and fill in its card: name, <b>alias</b> (a short prefix, e.g. <code>UF</code>), author, description and what is <b>public</b> (by default, its functions and scripts; what those need travels too, as private, and the examples do not travel). \"Save to GitHub\" leaves it in the <code>MyLibrary/</code> folder of the repository, with the extension <code>.alib.json</code> and a version that goes up by itself (v1, v2…); if it had been opened from another folder, it offers to send the original to the trash; \"Open from GitHub\" has the <b>Libraries</b> tab to reopen it. To use it in another document: <b>File → Insert library…</b>, choose it and confirm the alias. A block <code>▸ UtilidadesFatiga (UF) · v3</code> remains with a copy of its definitions (it works offline) and its names are used with the alias: <code>UF.C_(surf)(520'MPa, \"machined\")=</code>, <code>UF.Neuber_(steels)_(1,1)=</code>; the prefix is drawn in gray. It applies from its place on the page, like any definition, and its functions always use their own tables even if your document has others with the same name. If the library changes on GitHub, the block shows <code>⟳ v4</code>; in its properties (double tap), \"Update\" brings in the new version and first tells you which names are added or disappear. If the library uses another decimal separator, it is converted when inserted; if it uses another angle mode, the block warns you.</p>\n</details>\n</details>",
  "estilo": "<details data-ch=\"estilo\"><summary>Style, layers and view</summary>\n<p>Color, line type, width and fills; layers; what the view shows; and the element tree.</p>\n<details><summary>Style</summary>\n<p>Color, line type (continuous, hidden, axis, phantom, dashed, dotted, trace and point, or a new one with its own pattern) and width (thin, normal, thick). It applies to the selection and to new elements.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Fill / hatch</summary>\n<p>On a closed outline (lines, arcs or a circle): lines, cross-hatch or solid, with angle, spacing and opacity.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Layers</summary>\n<p><b>Layers</b> (top left corner of the drawing): opens the list of layers. At first there are none, and what you draw belongs to no layer. \"Create layer\" makes the new layer the <b>current layer</b> (checked circle): whatever you draw afterwards, including points, texts and fills, goes on it; the \"None\" row goes back to drawing with no layer. The eye <b>turns the layer off or on</b> (when it is off, its elements, the points used only by them, and their constraints and dimensions are not visible, cannot be selected, do not cut when trimming or extending, and are not exported; they still exist and the solver respects them). The trash can <b>deletes</b> the layer, and its elements become \"none\". You can rename a layer in its row. Turning off the current layer leaves you with no current layer. To change the layer of an element, double-tap it. <b>Dimensions</b> and <b>leaders</b> also have a layer (the current one when you create them; you change it in their window, with a double tap): with the layer off they are not visible and are not exported, but a dimension still measures or drives through its formula, for example one that moves a part from a calculation. Copy and paste, split and trim keep the layer of the original. When there is a <b>current layer</b>, its name appears under the Layers button.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>View</summary>\n<p>Grid, magnet (to the grid), soft lock (and every how many degrees), time for the snap to intersections, show or hide constraints, points, dimensions and texts, finger draws, panel labels, and dash scale. In the bottom left corner of the drawing you see in italics the <b>app version and its build</b>, the commit it was compiled from (for example <i>v1.6.1 7bc0108</i>; with \"+\" if it had uncommitted changes), so you know exactly which build each device is running. In the top right corner you see the <b>name of the current file</b> (or \"unnamed\").</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Element tree</summary>\n<p><b>Element tree</b> (first button of the bar): lists lines, circles, arcs, splines, texts, fills, points and relations. Tap an element to select it and see its points and constraints. Each thing has a single place of its own: a point lives in the first element that uses it (centers, in their circle) and appears in the others as a link <i>↗</i> (\"↗ Point p1 (in Circle c2)\"); under \"Loose points\" you only find those that belong to no element. Relations live in \"Relations and dimensions\", separated into Dimensions, Geometric, Mechanical and Automatic. The automatic ones are grouped by the operation that created them, numbered in the order it was done (\"Circular array 2\", \"Fillet 1\", \"Mirror copy 1\", \"Chamfer\", \"Split\", \"Polygon\"), and those of fixed shapes and vectors by their element; those with no origin (drawings made before this version, or those the magnet adds when drawing on an element) are grouped by type; under each element there is a single \"Relations (n)\" row with links to its own and to those of its points, which also counts the automatic ones. Tapping a link selects the element and opens its own place. If the drawing has layers, the layers button in the header groups the tree <b>by layer</b>: first each layer (empty ones too), with its eye to turn it on or off, and inside it the same groups by type; then \"No layer\" and the relations, actuators, graphs and paths, which have no layer. A point follows its element even if it is on another layer. The <b>Outlines</b> group joins the lines and arcs with stroke (not construction), elliptical arcs and open splines chained by their ends: \"Outline 2 · closed · 4 lines, 2 arcs\" (closed, open or branched if three or more meet at a node). Two ends touch if they are the same point, if a coincidence ties them, or if they are in the same place with nothing tying them: those are flagged (\"1 join without constraint\"), because they can open when the drawing is moved; to tie them, add a coincidence. Tapping an outline selects all its elements; inside it they appear as links, in the order they are traversed. If a free end is near another (within 1 % of the drawing size), the outline says how large that <b>gap</b> is and with which outline (\"gap 0.0143 with 2\"), or \"between its ends\" if it almost closes by itself: this is the typical reason a profile that should be closed comes out open.</p>\n</details>\n</details>",
  "archivo": "<details data-ch=\"archivo\"><summary>File and export</summary>\n<p>Saving and opening drawings (also in a GitHub repository) and exporting to PNG, SVG, DXF and SVG for laser cutting.</p>\n<details><summary>File button</summary>\n<p><b>File button</b> (bar, between the menu and help): opens the dropdown with New, Save, Open, PNG, SVG, SVG for laser cutting and DXF. They used to be in a section of the tools menu.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>GitHub</summary>\n<p><b>GitHub</b> (File → Save to GitHub / Open from GitHub / Set up GitHub): saves and opens .json files in a <b>private repository</b>, so you can continue a drawing on another device and keep the version history (each save is a commit). Setup, once per device: repository (<code>user/repository</code>), branch, folder and a <b>fine-grained personal access token</b> (GitHub → Settings → Developer settings → Fine-grained tokens) limited to that single repository with the \"Contents: Read and write\" permission. The token stays only in this browser's storage, does not go into the files or the .json; anyone with access to your browser could use it, which is why the minimum permission and a repository used only for drawings are advisable. If the file changed on GitHub since the last time you opened or saved it here (for example, you edited it on another device), the app asks before overwriting; there is no automatic merge: one version wins. It does not work offline (local saving is still available). The history is visible on GitHub, in the file's tab → History.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Managing GitHub files</summary>\n<p><b>GitHub files</b> (File → Open from GitHub): tapping a name opens it. Each row has a checkbox to <b>select several</b> (\"All\" / \"None\"); on the selection: <b>Trash</b> (moves to the repository's <code>_papelera</code> subfolder, does not delete), <b>Rename</b> (one at a time), <b>Duplicate</b> (\"name copia.json\") and <b>Protect</b> / \"Remove protection\". The <b>Trash</b> tab lets you <b>Restore</b> or <b>Delete permanently</b> (this one is irreversible from the app; the file remains in GitHub's git history). A <b>protected</b> file (🔒) asks for a second confirmation to go to the trash and another to be deleted completely; the mark is saved in the repository (<code>_ipad-cad.json</code>, inside the configured folder), so it applies on all your devices, and travels with the file when it is renamed or moved. Moving and renaming create the destination and then delete the original: if something fails halfway you are left with an extra copy, the file is never lost. Each operation is a commit; in large batches GitHub may throttle consecutive writes, and the notice lists what could not be done. Above the list appears <b>Current drawing</b>: the automatic save of this device (the time of the last save and how many elements it has), with \"Save to GitHub\" and \"Discard and start new\"; it exists only on this device and reopens by itself when the app starts. Each file shows the <b>date of its last change</b> (requested from GitHub in the background, one per file; a renamed or restored file counts from that move), there is a <b>search box</b> by name (ignoring case and accents; what the filter hides is not selected and does not receive actions) and the <b>Sort</b> button toggles name / date (most recent first).</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Save / Open</summary>\n<p>The drawing is saved to a .json file (it asks for the name). In addition, the app remembers the last drawing <b>only in this browser</b>: save a .json to back it up.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Device data</summary>\n<p><b>File → Data…</b> shows the data folder of this device, shared by all drawings: the texts saved by <code>wfile(valor, Nombre)</code> and read by <code>rfile(Nombre)</code>, and the audio files. <b>Import…</b> adds a file (an audio file is asked for with a name, is reduced to one channel, 16 kHz and 8 bits, about 16 KB per second, and plays with <code>play(\"nombre\")</code>). Each item can be <b>tested</b> (audio), <b>exported</b> and <b>deleted</b>. When a drawing is saved it carries the audio files it names in quotes (also inside a library) and, when opened, restores them if they are missing on the device, without overwriting one that already exists with that name (if it is different, it warns and uses the one on the device). A name built from a variable, such as <code>play(n)</code>, is not detected and does not travel with the drawing. The <code>wfile</code> texts do not travel with the drawing.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>PNG</summary>\n<p>Image of the drawing. <b>SVG</b>: vector. <b>DXF</b>: for CAD (dimensions come out as geometry and text).</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>SVG CUT</summary>\n<p><b>SVG CUT</b> (laser cutting): SVG at real scale in mm. Each <i>line class</i> (type, color and width) is sent to <b>Cut</b> (cut color, red by default), <b>Mark</b> (another color, for example folds) or <b>Skip</b>. It comes out in two groups, \"cut\" and \"mark\". Dimensions, texts, points and fills are not exported.</p>\n</details>\n</details>",
  "atajos": "<details data-ch=\"atajos\"><summary>Keyboard shortcuts</summary>\n<p>The keys for each tool, for working with the keyboard.</p>\n<p>Tools (one letter, without Ctrl):</p>\n<ul><li><b>V</b> select, <b>P</b> point, <b>I</b> intersection.</li><li><b>L</b> line, <b>K</b> construction line, <b>M</b> midpoint line, <b>W</b> vector.</li><li><b>C</b> circle, <b>O</b> ellipse, <b>R</b> arc by center, <b>A</b> arc by 3 points, <b>S</b> spline.</li><li><b>B</b> rectangle, <b>G</b> polygon.</li><li><b>T</b> text, <b>D</b> dimension.</li><li><b>X</b> trim, <b>E</b> extend, <b>F</b> fillet.</li></ul>\n<p>Editing:</p>\n<ul><li><b>Ctrl/Cmd+C</b> copy and <b>Ctrl/Cmd+V</b> paste.</li><li><b>Ctrl/Cmd+Z</b> undo, <b>Ctrl/Cmd+Y</b> (or <b>Ctrl/Cmd+Shift+Z</b>) redo.</li><li><b>Del</b> or <b>Backspace</b> deletes the selection; <b>Esc</b> cancels or finishes what you are doing.</li></ul>\n\n</details>",
  "limites": "<details data-ch=\"limites\"><summary>Known limits</summary>\n<p>What the app does not do yet or does only under certain conditions. Worth reading before a big job.</p>\n<ul><li>The ellipse does not support intersections or tangency with splines; where an ellipse crosses a spline or another ellipse, trimming or extending is computed on the drawn curve (a very close approximation, not exact).</li><li>Copies in a circular array and symmetries do not load the solver: a point that is only a copy of another is computed from its source after solving (the solver has fixed memory and ran out at about 220 copied points). If you add another constraint to a copy point, or use it in a curve with relationships (point on spline, tangency), it becomes an unknown again and counts against that limit.</li><li>A trimmed spline keeps its number of points and spaces them at equal distances over what remains (if the cut splits it in two, they are spaced according to the length of each piece): it looks very much like the original, but it is not identical. If the spline has few points and with them it would drift from the curve (by more than 0.1% of its size), the necessary ones are added. If it has relationships or any of its points is fixed or used by another element, the points do not move and only a few are added next to the cut.</li><li>The midpoint as a drawing snap is only offered on lines (not on arcs, ellipses or splines) and not on lines that are very short on screen.</li><li>Dimensioning: a spline cannot be dimensioned against an arc or circle (it has no center), and the distance between two parallel lines carries no sign.</li><li>A tangency with a spline may fail to converge if the contact point starts very far from the solution (the app starts it at the closest point).</li><li>Splitting arcs and circles into equal parts does not leave an equal-angle constraint.</li><li>The angular dimensions of a circular array cannot be edited by hand (the array is redone); the distance of a linear array can.</li><li>If the solver does not converge or there are conflicting constraints, the action is undone with a warning.</li><li>Layers: they only group elements (visibility and current layer); they have no color or line type of their own. A point that is part of an element follows that element's layer; changing the layer of a whole selection has to be done element by element, from its properties window.</li><li>A constraint or dimension that joins a visible element with a hidden one is also hidden while the layer is off.</li></ul>\n<details><summary>Graphs with simulation in Chrome on iPad</summary>\n<p>Computing a graph that simulates the mechanism (for example <code>p41.y vs p41.x</code> with actuators) can close the page in Chrome on iPad; in Safari, on PC and on the phone it works. Observed with the test drawing <code>prueba-simulaciones.json</code>: if the page closed while computing, on reopening the graphs are left \"paused\" with a notice of the progress reached, and computing again (one tap on the graph) can fail again, each time, with the drawing saved automatically; the same file opened from GitHub (Open) loads without problems. Moving or resizing the graph, and panning or zooming, do not fail (they do not recompute). The cause is not yet confirmed. In the meantime: open the file from GitHub instead of reopening the automatic save, or use Safari.</p>\n</details>\n</details>",
  "acerca": "<details data-ch=\"acerca\"><summary>About</summary>\n<p>The solver that resolves the constraints, the libraries the app uses, and where the version history is.</p>\n<p>The constraints are solved by <b>PlaneGCS</b>, the 2D geometric solver of the <i>Sketcher</i> module of <b>FreeCAD</b>, whose original code is by <b>Konstantinos Poulios</b> (© 2011, part of the FreeCAD project, LGPL license).</p>\n<p>The app uses it compiled to WebAssembly through the <b>@salusoft89/planegcs</b> package (v1.3.0), a wasm build and JavaScript wrapper by <b>Salusoft89</b>, licensed LGPL-2.0-or-later: <a href=\"https://github.com/Salusoft89/planegcs\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">github.com/Salusoft89/planegcs</a>. FreeCAD: <a href=\"https://www.freecad.org\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">freecad.org</a>.</p>\n<p>Splines are <b>native cubic B-splines of PlaneGCS</b>: each spline point lies on its knot, the poles are auxiliary unknowns of the solver, and the drawn curve is the same one the solver resolves. The point on spline (<code>point_on_bspline</code>) and the tangencies with lines, circles and arcs (<code>angle_via_point_and_param</code>) are solved exactly. In open splines, zero curvature at the ends is expressed with a midpoint constraint between poles.</p>\n<p>Other components: <b>Lucide</b> icons (ISC license) and packaging with <b>Vite</b> (MIT license).</p>\n<details><summary>Version history</summary>\n<p>The detail of each version is in <a href=\"https://github.com/oscampo/a-cad/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CHANGELOG.md</a>, and the complete manual in <a href=\"https://github.com/oscampo/a-cad/tree/main/docs\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">docs/</a>.</p>\n</details>\n</details>"
 }
};
